"In Lesson 1, a row of switches could show a number, and nothing else. But a computer shows you words and photos, and it plays music. How can numbers do all that? The answer is an agreement. People decide that a certain number will stand for a certain thing, and they write the agreement down in a table. Such a table is called a code. Once everyone uses the same code, any computer can turn numbers back into the same letters, colors, and sounds.": "5cb077f168df",
"In Lesson 1, a row of switches could show a number, and nothing else. But a computer shows you words and photos, and it plays music. How can numbers do all that? The answer is an agreement. People decide that a certain number will stand for a certain thing, and they write the agreement down in a table. Such a table is called a code. Once everyone uses the same code, any computer can turn numbers back into the same letters, colors, and sounds. Start with letters. In 1963, American computer makers agreed on a code called ASCII. It uses seven bits, so it has room for 128 characters: the English letters, the digits, punctuation marks, and a few signals for machines. In ASCII, the capital letter A is number 65, B is 66, and a lowercase a is 97. When you press A on a keyboard, the computer does not store the shape of the letter. It stores 65, which in bits is 1000001. But 128 characters are not enough for the world. For years, different places made their own codes, and the same number could mean different characters on different computers. Text opened with the wrong code turned into nonsense. Unicode fixes this with one giant table for every writing system. The 2026 version, Unicode 18.0, lists 172,808 characters. The Chinese character 永 is number 27,704, usually written U+6C38. Most web pages store these numbers with a method called UTF-8, which uses one byte for an English letter and three bytes for a common Chinese character such as 永. Pictures work the same way. A picture on a screen is a grid of tiny squares called pixels, and each pixel has just one color. That color is stored as three numbers: how much red, how much green, and how much blue. Each number usually gets one byte, so it runs from 0 to 255. Red 255, green 0, blue 0 is pure red. Three bytes give 16,777,216 possible colors. A photo 4,000 pixels wide and 3,000 pixels tall has 12 million pixels, so before any shrinking it needs 36 million bytes. Sound becomes numbers, too. A microphone turns sound into an electrical wave that rises and falls. A computer measures the height of that wave again and again, at equal steps of time, and writes down each measurement as a number. This is called sampling. For a music CD, the wave is measured 44,100 times every second, and each measurement is a 16-bit number. To play the music, the computer reads the numbers back in order and a speaker rebuilds the wave. Here is the surprising part: the bits themselves do not say what they are. The eight bits 01000001 can be the number 65, the letter A, or a dim shade of red. Nothing in the bits tells you which. The meaning comes from the code that the computer uses to read them. That is why a photo opened as if it were text looks like nonsense. Everything in a computer is numbers, plus an agreement about what the numbers mean.": "eb8132c99d3c",
